# THE FUSIONS WHOSE PARTS ARE NOT DISJOINT, WITH THE LAYER THEY BOTH REACH. One row per
# (fusion, layer reached through two of its own parts), carrying which two parts got there.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ A FUSION'S PARTS CLAIM TO PARTITION THE COMPOSED LAYER, AND THIS IS WHERE THAT CLAIM
#    IS FALSE. Not a duplicate: a layer somebody referred to at one level while a sibling part
#    already carried it, so the partition has an overlap nobody wrapped. `asrt:Elimination` is
#    the filer ADMITTING an overlap and sizing it. This is an overlap that is structural,
#    visible in the incidence, and unadmitted.
#
# ⭐⭐ THE SUBJECT IS THE FUSION, NOT THE ROOT ABOVE IT. Every layer above the fusion also
#    reaches the doubled layer twice, so a rule keyed on the root reports one defect once per
#    ancestor and names a composer who did nothing wrong. The composer at fault is the one
#    holding both parts, which is exactly the key here. Measured on a three-level probe: keyed
#    on the root this returns two rows against two filings, keyed on the fusion it returns one.
#
# ⛔ THE REACH IS REFLEXIVE ON PURPOSE. Naming a layer directly while a sibling composes it is
#   the canonical case, so a part must count as reaching ITSELF or the commonest form of this
#   escapes. `merge-holding-composition/compute` naming `merge-us-member/compute` beside
#   `merge-group-composition/compute-us` is that case, and it is in this corpus: the holding
#   reaches one member's layer directly and reaches it again through the group that composes it.
#   ⛔ A population written as the parts a fusion does NOT name cannot see it, because the
#   reflexive reach is a part the fusion DOES name.
#
# ⛔⛔ AT ONE LEVEL THE PRIMARY KEY ON `pm.part` ALREADY REFUSES THIS AND NOTHING CAN AT TWO,
#    because the second path runs through a document the first does not contain. That is the
#    whole reason this is a query. ⚠️ It is NOT conditioned on a filed elimination: the key
#    governs the redeclaration and `e` governs the number, and folding an answer about a
#    magnitude into a structural population is how a rule stops being able to see the structure.
#
# ⭐ THE PAIR COMPARISON KEEPS IT A SET OF UNORDERED PAIRS and drops the reflexive one, so a
#   part is never reported against itself.
-- composition/parts.sqlc self-joined on the fusion, against the reflexive closure of
-- composition/descent.sqlc, for the layer two sibling parts both reach.
SELECT DISTINCT
       a.composition    AS filing,
       a.composed_layer AS layer,
       r1.filing        AS doubled_filing,
       r1.layer         AS doubled_layer,
       a.part_filing    AS via_filing,
       a.part_layer     AS via_layer,
       b.part_filing    AS also_via_filing,
       b.part_layer     AS also_via_layer
FROM      (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) a
JOIN      (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) b ON  b.composition    = a.composition
    AND b.composed_layer = a.composed_layer
    AND (a.part_filing, a.part_layer) < (b.part_filing, b.part_layer)
JOIN      (
    SELECT DISTINCT root_filing, root_layer, filing, layer
    FROM (
        :compose(composition/descent.sqlc)
    ) w
  UNION
    SELECT DISTINCT part_filing, part_layer, part_filing, part_layer
    FROM (
        :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
    ) p
) r1 ON r1.root_filing = a.part_filing AND r1.root_layer = a.part_layer
JOIN      (
    SELECT DISTINCT root_filing, root_layer, filing, layer
    FROM (
        :compose(composition/descent.sqlc)
    ) w
  UNION
    SELECT DISTINCT part_filing, part_layer, part_filing, part_layer
    FROM (
        :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
    ) p
) r2 ON  r2.root_filing = b.part_filing AND r2.root_layer = b.part_layer
     AND r2.filing = r1.filing AND r2.layer = r1.layer
